[
  {"id":"LEG-1","kind":"priority","severity":"blocking","requirement":"R-25","rule":"LD-08","title":"Export of personal data is deferred to LATER, so the app cannot export a person's data","why":"R-25 reads '[LATER] Export and import of the data as JSON', and no MUST produces a file. R-03 only shows fields on screen. LD-08 requires 'An export for the person is structured, commonly used and machine-readable' and says 'a PDF, a screenshot or a printed table is not machine-readable, a JSON or CSV file is'. Article 20(1) is the source. R-25 also describes the whole data set, which would hand one person the data of every other customer, so moving it to MUST is not enough. The app as specified holds personal data it cannot export.","suggested":"### R-28 [MUST] Export one customer's data\n- From the customer detail, the user can download a JSON file holding that customer's fields and all their orders with their lines.\n- The file holds no data of any other customer.\n\n### R-25 [LATER] Import of the data, and export of the whole data set, as JSON","proven":true},
  {"id":"LEG-2","kind":"missing","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-03","rule":"LD-06","title":"Access needs more than the data: purpose and retention are not part of what the person gets","why":"R-03 reads 'The customer detail shows all fields and the customer's orders with their totals', which is a screen, not a copy. LD-06 says the right of access needs a path in the app: 'which data is held, for what purpose, how long', with 'the copy ... provided in a commonly used electronic form, within one month of the request'. The file itself is LEG-1. This item adds the purpose and the retention to it.","suggested":"Add to R-28:\n- The file also states the purpose of the data (A-11) and how long it is kept (A-12), so the user can answer a person's access request with this one file.","proven":true},
  {"id":"LEG-3","kind":"wrong","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-04","rule":"LD-07","title":"The refusal to delete a customer rests on no listed exception and does not say how to erase","why":"R-04 reads 'A customer who has orders cannot be deleted; the app says why and how many orders block it, and no data changes.' LD-07 reads 'a refusal rests on a listed exception and the app says which'. Having orders is neither of the exceptions the rule names: 17(3)(b) legal obligation or 17(3)(e) legal claim. A-10 rules out issued invoices, so no stored invoice blocks erasure either. It is advisory because a route exists: R-08 deletes the orders, then R-04 deletes the customer. Nothing in the app tells the user that route. The human chose this rule for R-04, so the suggestion keeps it and only adds the way out.","suggested":"Replace the second criterion of R-04:\n- A customer who has orders is not deleted while those orders exist; the app says how many orders block it, that no legal retention applies, and that deleting those orders (R-08) and then the customer erases all of this customer's data. No data changes at this step.","proven":true},
  {"id":"LEG-4","kind":"missing","severity":"advisory","requirement":"none","rule":"LD-02","title":"No personal data field has a stated purpose","why":"The data model lists Customer 'name string, email string, phone string, address string' and Order 'notes string', and A-02 defines them. No requirement or assumption says what each is for. LD-02 reads 'Every personal data field has a stated purpose and is used only for it' and 'a field with no purpose has no legal ground to exist'. Under LD-01 these fields are personal data, as is 'a customer number joined to a name', which covers Customer.id and Order.customerId. Order.notes and OrderLine.description are free text and can also hold personal data.","suggested":"- **A-11** Purpose of personal data: `name`, `email`, `phone` and `address` identify and contact the customer an order belongs to; `id` and `customerId` link orders to that customer; `notes` and line `description` record what the order is about and should not hold details about other people. The data is used for nothing else.","proven":true},
  {"id":"LEG-5","kind":"wrong","severity":"advisory","requirement":"none","rule":"LD-03","title":"Fields that no requirement needs: createdAt, and phone and address as far as the flows go","why":"The data model has 'createdAt ISO string' on Customer and on Order. No requirement reads either: R-03 does not show it and R-09 sorts by 'newest date first, then highest number first'. On Customer it is a fact about an identifiable person. Phone and address appear only as edit and display fields (R-02, R-03), and R-01 names only 'a name ... and an optional email'. No flow uses them. LD-03 reads 'The app stores only the personal data its purpose needs, by default' and warns against 'a field \"because it may be useful later\"'.","suggested":"In the data model sketch, remove `createdAt` from Customer and Order unless a requirement uses it.\nChange A-02: **A-02** Customer fields: name (required), email, phone, address (all optional, free text up to 200 characters, empty by default). Keep phone and address only if the human confirms the purpose in A-11; otherwise remove them.","proven":true},
  {"id":"LEG-6","kind":"missing","severity":"advisory","requirement":"none","rule":"LD-05","title":"No retention rule for Customer or Order","why":"A-09 reads 'Deleting is permanent; there is no undo or trash', and that is the only thing said about how data ends. The Customer and Order entities have no end date. LD-05 reads 'every entity with personal data has a retention rule' and 'a customer record with no end date is kept forever by default'. No domain skill exists in the project to carry it, and the period is a human decision.","suggested":"- **A-12** Retention: a customer and their orders are kept until the user deletes them, and no later than NEEDS HUMAN: the period after the customer's last order. The app deletes nothing by itself; the user applies this rule with R-04 and R-08. No legal retention applies while the app issues no invoices (A-10).","proven":true},
  {"id":"LEG-7","kind":"missing","severity":"advisory","requirement":"none","rule":"LD-10","title":"The data model does not name its personal data categories, purposes and retention","why":"The 'Data model sketch' lists fields only. LD-10 reads 'The categories of personal data, their purposes and their retention are written down' and 'the requirements and the data model are that somewhere, and a reviewer can check them'. Purposes and retention are LEG-4 and LEG-6. This item puts the record where a reviewer looks for it.","suggested":"Add under the data model sketch:\nPersonal data: Customer `id`, `name`, `email`, `phone`, `address`; Order `customerId`, `notes`; OrderLine `description` when it names a person. Purpose: A-11. Retention: A-12.","proven":true},
  {"id":"LEG-8","kind":"missing","severity":"advisory","requirement":"none","rule":"LD-09","title":"No requirement keeps personal data out of error text, URLs and the page title","why":"LD-09 reads 'Personal data does not leak into logs, error messages, URLs or the storage of a shared machine'. AGENTS.md rule 6 bans `console.log` under `src/`, which covers logs. Nothing covers the rest. R-10 adds a search by customer name and R-03 a customer detail, and neither says what the address or the title carries. R-17, R-20 and R-21 add error text. No requirement line breaks the rule today. What settles it is this criterion, or the URL scheme in docs/UI-SPEC.md, which does not exist yet.","suggested":"### R-29 [MUST] Personal data stays out of errors, URLs and titles\n- Error messages, the page title and the URL never contain a customer's name, email, phone or address, or the text typed into a search; a page is addressed by `id` only.","proven":false},
  {"id":"LEG-9","kind":"unclear","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-19","rule":"LD-09","title":"Personal data sits in plain localStorage, and the requirements do not say the machine is private","why":"R-19 reads 'All data is stored under one localStorage key as JSON', and the brief mandates localStorage, so the storage itself is a given. LD-09 names 'data left in localStorage on a shared computer' as a disclosure. A-01 reads 'One user, one browser, no accounts', which says one user but not that the machine is theirs. There is no lock and no way to clear everything. R-20 keeps unreadable data and refuses to write over it, so in that state the app offers no way to erase it. ADR-002 sends delivery to GitHub Pages, where a project site shares its origin, and so its localStorage, with the owner's other sites under the same github.io name. To settle it: the human states whether the app may run on a shared machine, and the devops decision names the origin.","suggested":"Change A-01: **A-01** One user, one browser, no accounts, on a device only that user can open. Data stays on the device; nothing is sent anywhere.\n\n### R-30 [MUST] Erase all data on this device\n- After a confirmation that says all customers and orders will be gone for good, the user can remove the app's stored data, also when the stored data is unreadable (R-20).","proven":false},
  {"id":"LEG-10","kind":"missing","severity":"advisory","requirement":"none","rule":"LL-11","title":"The project has no LICENSE file and no decision on its licence","why":"The repository root holds no LICENSE file, and AGENTS.md does not list one in its structure. LL-11 reads 'The project has a LICENSE file' and 'without a LICENSE file the project itself is all rights reserved (LL-02), so nobody, not even a colleague, may legally use it'. AGENTS.md cites docs/decisions/ADR-001-stack.md, but that file does not exist yet, so the skill's question whether ADR-001 says what the licence is has no answer. No third-party library, font or icon set is named: AGENTS.md says 'no dependencies'. So LL-01 to LL-10 have nothing to bite on until docs/UI-SPEC.md exists.","suggested":"- **A-13** The project ships a `LICENSE` file at the root, and its licence is named by SPDX identifier in ADR-001 (NEEDS HUMAN: the licence). Any library, font, icon set or adapted design added later is named with its SPDX identifier in a decision first.","proven":true},
  {"id":"LEG-11","kind":"risk","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-23","rule":"LE-01","title":"The LATER invoice line carries obligations the current model and flows would not meet","why":"A-10 puts legal invoices out of scope, so LE-01 to LE-08 break nothing today. R-23 is a heading with no criteria, though, and the choices made now would collide with it later. The data model has no supplier, VAT number, tax or supply date, while LE-01 says 'A data model that cannot hold one of them cannot issue a lawful invoice'. R-07, R-12 and R-08 edit and delete orders, while the LE-03 review asks whether lines can be 'changed through the order'. R-04 and A-09 delete freely, while LE-07 reads 'Retention beats erasure'. To settle it: the human confirms that the app's output is never used as an invoice, or adds these criteria when R-23 is taken up.","suggested":"### R-23 [LATER] Issue invoices (numbered, immutable, printable or PDF)\n- Before this is built: the data model holds every Article 226 detail, the numbering is sequential and unique, an issued invoice is read-only and independent of later order edits, a correction is a new document, the retention period is named in a decision, erasure respects it, and the customer's acceptance of electronic invoices is recorded.","proven":false}
